00:00:06.548 --> 00:00:07.590
RETO MEIER: Hi, everyone.

00:00:07.590 --> 00:00:09.507
Thanks for joining us
on the "Next Live Show."

00:00:09.507 --> 00:00:10.240
I'm Reto Meier.

00:00:10.240 --> 00:00:11.390
STEPHANIE WONG: And
I'm Stephanie Wong.

00:00:11.390 --> 00:00:12.920
If you can't be here
in person this week,

00:00:12.920 --> 00:00:13.910
we've got you covered.

00:00:13.910 --> 00:00:15.410
To make sure you
catch everything,

00:00:15.410 --> 00:00:17.210
we have six livestream
channels, including

00:00:17.210 --> 00:00:19.202
this one, the "Next Live Show."

00:00:19.202 --> 00:00:20.660
Let's kick things
off with insights

00:00:20.660 --> 00:00:22.850
on this morning's keynote
from Google Cloud leaders

00:00:22.850 --> 00:00:26.180
Aparna Sinha, group product
manager Kubernetes, and Quentin

00:00:26.180 --> 00:00:27.530
Hardy, head of Editorial.

00:00:27.530 --> 00:00:28.452
Great to see you both.

00:00:28.452 --> 00:00:29.910
APARNA SINHA: Thanks
for having us.

00:00:29.910 --> 00:00:31.460
STEPHANIE WONG: Quentin,
we were just here together

00:00:31.460 --> 00:00:34.370
nine months ago talking about
the state of cloud computing,

00:00:34.370 --> 00:00:35.623
and that wasn't very long ago.

00:00:35.623 --> 00:00:37.040
What have you seen
that's changed?

00:00:37.040 --> 00:00:38.748
QUENTIN HARDY: That's
cloud time for you.

00:00:38.748 --> 00:00:39.950
Nine months equals a year.

00:00:39.950 --> 00:00:41.690
And two things stick out.

00:00:41.690 --> 00:00:43.280
One, obviously, new leadership.

00:00:43.280 --> 00:00:44.930
We have a new leader
at Google Cloud.

00:00:44.930 --> 00:00:46.520
And the first keynote
is very telling.

00:00:46.520 --> 00:00:50.870
He's focused on customer-ready,
enterprise-ready products

00:00:50.870 --> 00:00:52.790
and services, and
a wealth of them,

00:00:52.790 --> 00:00:54.770
and customer testimonials
backing that up,

00:00:54.770 --> 00:00:55.855
which is fun to see.

00:00:55.855 --> 00:00:57.230
The other thing
is the products--

00:00:57.230 --> 00:00:58.160
a wealth of products.

00:00:58.160 --> 00:01:00.200
A year's worth of
products in nine months.

00:01:00.200 --> 00:01:02.510
I'd like to just cite one
of them, the Cloud Services

00:01:02.510 --> 00:01:05.630
Platform, which is
now called Anthos.

00:01:05.630 --> 00:01:10.250
Last summer, when it was
launched, it was prospective.

00:01:10.250 --> 00:01:12.840
It was going to be in alpha
and then beta very rapidly.

00:01:12.840 --> 00:01:16.460
It's ready in nine months with
more features, and products,

00:01:16.460 --> 00:01:18.830
and services within it
than we could imagine

00:01:18.830 --> 00:01:20.030
having done this quickly.

00:01:20.030 --> 00:01:22.070
Yay to your team.

00:01:22.070 --> 00:01:28.550
And what this allows is for you
to create, deploy, update, run,

00:01:28.550 --> 00:01:32.390
check, secure all these
applications on-prem,

00:01:32.390 --> 00:01:35.150
in Google Cloud, on all the
clouds-- wherever you want.

00:01:35.150 --> 00:01:37.340
Just take the asset
and make it grow

00:01:37.340 --> 00:01:40.940
and make it live better
in a system that is open,

00:01:40.940 --> 00:01:43.940
and secure, and aimed
at customer needs.

00:01:43.940 --> 00:01:46.190
RETO MEIER: Now, Aparna,
you've been closely involved

00:01:46.190 --> 00:01:48.650
in the creation of the
Cloud Services Platform,

00:01:48.650 --> 00:01:50.240
or Anthos, now.

00:01:50.240 --> 00:01:52.320
Based on what TK
has already shared,

00:01:52.320 --> 00:01:53.770
it already does a lot, right?

00:01:53.770 --> 00:01:55.550
But it's going to do even more.

00:01:55.550 --> 00:01:57.620
So what will that look
like, and why should that

00:01:57.620 --> 00:01:58.678
matter to our customers?

00:01:58.678 --> 00:01:59.470
APARNA SINHA: Yeah.

00:01:59.470 --> 00:02:01.370
Well, you know, great
computing platforms

00:02:01.370 --> 00:02:05.600
don't just add features
or reduce cost.

00:02:05.600 --> 00:02:07.280
Great computing
platforms actually

00:02:07.280 --> 00:02:08.900
change the game entirely.

00:02:08.900 --> 00:02:12.780
They give customers capabilities
that they've not had before.

00:02:12.780 --> 00:02:14.840
And that's what Anthos is.

00:02:14.840 --> 00:02:18.200
Anthos can be used, even
if you're not in the cloud.

00:02:18.200 --> 00:02:20.750
So it can be used in
your on-prem data center

00:02:20.750 --> 00:02:24.020
to give you a cloud-like
environment, cloud-like agility

00:02:24.020 --> 00:02:28.880
and speed, but also cloud-like
control and centralized risk

00:02:28.880 --> 00:02:30.140
management.

00:02:30.140 --> 00:02:33.950
And it gives both your
operators and your developers

00:02:33.950 --> 00:02:37.280
a level of visibility,
operational visibility,

00:02:37.280 --> 00:02:40.220
as well as ease of use
for the developer that

00:02:40.220 --> 00:02:42.368
is hard to find anywhere else.

00:02:42.368 --> 00:02:43.910
And then if you do
decide that you're

00:02:43.910 --> 00:02:46.880
going to use a
public cloud, Anthos

00:02:46.880 --> 00:02:50.060
gives you what no one else
gives you, which is choice.

00:02:50.060 --> 00:02:53.360
And it allows you to write
once and deploy your services

00:02:53.360 --> 00:02:54.470
anywhere.

00:02:54.470 --> 00:02:57.110
And that's what really
accelerates your business

00:02:57.110 --> 00:02:58.020
success.

00:02:58.020 --> 00:02:59.450
QUENTIN HARDY:
The reason we like

00:02:59.450 --> 00:03:01.250
open systems-- yes,
it's a better way

00:03:01.250 --> 00:03:03.170
to make more secure software.

00:03:03.170 --> 00:03:04.550
It's better choice.

00:03:04.550 --> 00:03:05.840
You get more velocity.

00:03:05.840 --> 00:03:07.520
These are all great advantages.

00:03:07.520 --> 00:03:09.920
There's an element of
self-interest here too, though.

00:03:09.920 --> 00:03:11.360
By leaving it up
to the customer,

00:03:11.360 --> 00:03:13.250
by giving them that
freedom and choice,

00:03:13.250 --> 00:03:15.980
it compels us to
stay competitive.

00:03:15.980 --> 00:03:17.390
We have to listen more closely.

00:03:17.390 --> 00:03:19.360
We have to respond more quickly.

00:03:19.360 --> 00:03:21.140
And if you have
locked-in customers,

00:03:21.140 --> 00:03:23.208
it's kind of a recipe
for mediocrity.

00:03:23.208 --> 00:03:25.250
APARNA SINHA: You know,
I think one of the things

00:03:25.250 --> 00:03:29.030
that a lot of customers
like about the cloud

00:03:29.030 --> 00:03:32.150
is that they don't have to
worry about all of the things.

00:03:32.150 --> 00:03:33.980
They can focus on
the things that

00:03:33.980 --> 00:03:36.110
are important to
them and things that

00:03:36.110 --> 00:03:38.040
add value to their business.

00:03:38.040 --> 00:03:41.000
So for example,
developers and engineers

00:03:41.000 --> 00:03:45.170
can focus on differentiated
capabilities that are unique.

00:03:45.170 --> 00:03:47.990
And the cloud takes
care of all the rest.

00:03:47.990 --> 00:03:49.670
But why only in the cloud?

00:03:49.670 --> 00:03:51.170
You know, this is
something that you

00:03:51.170 --> 00:03:53.330
ought to be able to
do on-prem as well.

00:03:53.330 --> 00:03:54.710
And that's what Anthos does.

00:03:54.710 --> 00:03:57.680
It dissolves the layers that are
underneath the differentiating

00:03:57.680 --> 00:03:58.460
pieces.

00:03:58.460 --> 00:04:01.490
And those are managed by
cloud, by Google Cloud.

00:04:01.490 --> 00:04:03.510
Or they're managed
automatically.

00:04:03.510 --> 00:04:06.050
And that way, you
know, your business

00:04:06.050 --> 00:04:08.408
can focus on
accelerating itself.

00:04:08.408 --> 00:04:09.325
STEPHANIE WONG: Right.

00:04:09.325 --> 00:04:11.200
It seems like it's
picking the workloads that

00:04:11.200 --> 00:04:12.663
make sense for our customers.

00:04:12.663 --> 00:04:14.330
Quentin, you mentioned
before we started

00:04:14.330 --> 00:04:17.029
that modernizing and migrating
was another big keynote

00:04:17.029 --> 00:04:17.839
takeaway.

00:04:17.839 --> 00:04:19.579
How does Google Cloud's
integration with

00:04:19.579 --> 00:04:22.735
and migration of enterprise
systems help our customers?

00:04:22.735 --> 00:04:24.860
QUENTIN HARDY: Exactly what
Aparna was just saying,

00:04:24.860 --> 00:04:26.490
why can't it do more?

00:04:26.490 --> 00:04:28.880
And in this case, why
can't your data do more?

00:04:28.880 --> 00:04:31.370
Why can't all that
hard work that people

00:04:31.370 --> 00:04:32.760
established over decades--

00:04:32.760 --> 00:04:34.760
learning their customers,
learning their market,

00:04:34.760 --> 00:04:37.310
learning their products--
be taken to the cloud

00:04:37.310 --> 00:04:41.090
or be empowered inside on-prem
systems and get new life,

00:04:41.090 --> 00:04:45.470
get new power, find new
insights with ML and AI,

00:04:45.470 --> 00:04:48.800
or by employing
other cloud services,

00:04:48.800 --> 00:04:51.770
just refreshing all that,
and taking that legacy,

00:04:51.770 --> 00:04:54.283
and using that as an insight
to move into the future?

00:04:54.283 --> 00:04:56.450
STEPHANIE WONG: Speaking
of this focus on our users,

00:04:56.450 --> 00:04:57.908
Quentin, what do
you think are some

00:04:57.908 --> 00:05:00.500
of the most compelling
examples of learnings

00:05:00.500 --> 00:05:02.407
that come with working
with our customers?

00:05:02.407 --> 00:05:04.490
QUENTIN HARDY: You know
what's easy to do in tech?

00:05:04.490 --> 00:05:06.340
Deliver a lot of complexity.

00:05:06.340 --> 00:05:08.710
And some companies
love complexity

00:05:08.710 --> 00:05:10.262
because it's kind
of like lock-in.

00:05:10.262 --> 00:05:12.470
If you can get somebody to
learn all that complexity,

00:05:12.470 --> 00:05:13.600
they'll never learn
anything else.

00:05:13.600 --> 00:05:14.470
They'll be afraid.

00:05:14.470 --> 00:05:15.370
You know what's hard?

00:05:15.370 --> 00:05:16.420
Simplicity.

00:05:16.420 --> 00:05:19.222
Simplicity on the back
end for the provider

00:05:19.222 --> 00:05:20.680
is really tough,
because you've got

00:05:20.680 --> 00:05:22.462
to consider a lot of use cases.

00:05:22.462 --> 00:05:23.920
You've got to build
in a way that's

00:05:23.920 --> 00:05:25.878
going to work for a lot
of different interests,

00:05:25.878 --> 00:05:27.280
and people, and locations.

00:05:27.280 --> 00:05:29.500
That requires a lot
of careful thought.

00:05:29.500 --> 00:05:32.140
And you've got to have a
kind of empathy for what

00:05:32.140 --> 00:05:33.573
the customer wants to do.

00:05:33.573 --> 00:05:34.990
What does the
customer want to do?

00:05:34.990 --> 00:05:37.240
The customer wants to do
the things it's good at.

00:05:37.240 --> 00:05:39.943
The customer-- he or
she wants to proceed

00:05:39.943 --> 00:05:41.860
with the things they've
learned in the market,

00:05:41.860 --> 00:05:43.960
with the products
they're passionate about.

00:05:43.960 --> 00:05:46.163
That might not actually
be container management.

00:05:46.163 --> 00:05:47.830
That's something we're
passionate about.

00:05:47.830 --> 00:05:49.310
That's something we do.

00:05:49.310 --> 00:05:52.210
And in some ways,
this is economics 101.

00:05:52.210 --> 00:05:55.300
Let everybody focus on
their comparative advantage.

00:05:55.300 --> 00:05:56.860
We are freeing up customers.

00:05:56.860 --> 00:05:58.720
We are giving them
that simplicity

00:05:58.720 --> 00:06:00.250
so they can get
stuff out of the way

00:06:00.250 --> 00:06:03.180
and just work on the problems
to serve their customers.

00:06:03.180 --> 00:06:04.930
RETO MEIER: Aparna,
Quentin's been talking

00:06:04.930 --> 00:06:06.132
a lot about business folks.

00:06:06.132 --> 00:06:07.840
But what does this
mean for the operators

00:06:07.840 --> 00:06:10.460
and the developers who are going
to be using this technology?

00:06:10.460 --> 00:06:11.252
APARNA SINHA: Yeah.

00:06:11.252 --> 00:06:13.120
Well, like I said,
it's a game changer

00:06:13.120 --> 00:06:14.860
for the operators
and the developers.

00:06:14.860 --> 00:06:17.600
I would say there are
three kind of big changes.

00:06:17.600 --> 00:06:20.620
Number one is the right
ones deploy anywhere.

00:06:20.620 --> 00:06:23.650
For developers,
it means that you

00:06:23.650 --> 00:06:26.560
can focus on the things
that add differentiation

00:06:26.560 --> 00:06:27.820
to your business.

00:06:27.820 --> 00:06:31.360
For the CIO, it means,
actually, that you can really

00:06:31.360 --> 00:06:33.130
concentrate your talent.

00:06:33.130 --> 00:06:36.760
You don't fragment your talent
across multiple different code

00:06:36.760 --> 00:06:40.090
bases, across multiple
different types of tools.

00:06:40.090 --> 00:06:41.590
And if you think
about it, you know,

00:06:41.590 --> 00:06:44.200
every customer I've talked
to always says, the talent

00:06:44.200 --> 00:06:45.880
is the most scarce resource.

00:06:45.880 --> 00:06:48.670
Particularly, engineering
talent is really hard to hire.

00:06:48.670 --> 00:06:52.580
It's really hard to keep
the talent motivated.

00:06:52.580 --> 00:06:54.040
And I think the
reason creators are

00:06:54.040 --> 00:06:56.830
excited about Anthos is, again,
for the same reasons, right?

00:06:56.830 --> 00:07:00.520
Because they also get access
to a multi-cloud platform.

00:07:00.520 --> 00:07:04.030
They also get access to the
benefits of something that's

00:07:04.030 --> 00:07:06.670
at a level that they can
differentiate their code

00:07:06.670 --> 00:07:09.530
and don't have to worry about
the underlying infrastructure

00:07:09.530 --> 00:07:10.868
pieces.

00:07:10.868 --> 00:07:12.910
STEPHANIE WONG: Quentin,
Google CEO Sundar Pichai

00:07:12.910 --> 00:07:16.130
was an important part of
the day 1 keynote as well.

00:07:16.130 --> 00:07:18.040
What did his presence
say about how

00:07:18.040 --> 00:07:19.882
Google sees the cloud industry?

00:07:19.882 --> 00:07:21.340
QUENTIN HARDY:
Well, obviously, you

00:07:21.340 --> 00:07:25.090
bring out the CEO of Google,
he's putting the stamp on it

00:07:25.090 --> 00:07:26.510
that this is big.

00:07:26.510 --> 00:07:27.760
And you can't say that enough.

00:07:27.760 --> 00:07:30.250
And this gets kind of to an
abstract reality of where

00:07:30.250 --> 00:07:32.260
we are right now in the world.

00:07:32.260 --> 00:07:35.620
There are four
major players, five.

00:07:35.620 --> 00:07:37.180
You could go to six.

00:07:37.180 --> 00:07:39.710
$5 to $10 billion in Capex each.

00:07:39.710 --> 00:07:41.800
This is a very big
stakes operation.

00:07:41.800 --> 00:07:45.400
Because this is the
infrastructure of the future.

00:07:45.400 --> 00:07:48.190
As much as you think about
airports, or highways,

00:07:48.190 --> 00:07:51.700
or deep water ports, or
telephones and telegraphs,

00:07:51.700 --> 00:07:55.390
in their time, this is the
infrastructure of the future

00:07:55.390 --> 00:07:57.910
that we are building and our
competitors are building.

00:07:57.910 --> 00:08:00.880
We're building it with a certain
spirit we've talked about today

00:08:00.880 --> 00:08:03.790
we think will give the
customers the most power,

00:08:03.790 --> 00:08:08.080
give individuals of any size-- a
small company, a large company,

00:08:08.080 --> 00:08:09.940
freelancer, you name it--

00:08:09.940 --> 00:08:12.670
greater energy, greater
power, greater freedom

00:08:12.670 --> 00:08:15.320
to realize what they
want to do in the world.

00:08:15.320 --> 00:08:16.240
STEPHANIE WONG: Thank you
so much for joining us

00:08:16.240 --> 00:08:17.320
again and sharing your insight.

00:08:17.320 --> 00:08:17.830
QUENTIN HARDY: It's
great to be here.

00:08:17.830 --> 00:08:18.440
APARNA SINHA: Thank you.

00:08:18.440 --> 00:08:19.470
Thanks for having us.

00:08:19.470 --> 00:08:22.520
[MUSIC PLAYING]

